Inteligeo, load test of 2026-11-11/12 (no advisory, no upstream fix): reading a
layer from GeoServer no longer resets GeoServer.

Target: GeoNode 4.3.1 as installed by this image (site-packages/geonode). Applied
with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` from site-packages (paths are geonode/...).

What happened: get_dataset (geoserver/helpers.py) and GeoServerResourceManager.exists
(geoserver/manager.py) called gs_catalog.reset() before reading a layer.
Catalog.reset() of geoserver-restconfig does two things: POST /rest/reset, which
makes GeoServer drop its store, raster and schema caches and the connections of every
store, and the clearing of the client's own 5-second cache of REST answers. In the
load test GeoServer logged "DataSource not available after calling dispose()" 937
times in one morning, in bursts that came with the uploads. That the reset under
requests in flight is the cause is inferred from the coincidence, not proved.

The change: both places keep only the clearing of the client cache
(gs_catalog._cache.clear()).

Who relied on the reset, read before removing it:
- What the two functions return comes from GET /rest/layers/<name>.xml, GeoServer's
  configuration, which /rest/reset does not touch; only the client cache could make
  them read an old answer, and that one is still cleared.
- get_dataset is called only by clean_styles and set_styles (same file). clean_styles
  resets GeoServer itself right before calling it, and set_styles ends by calling
  clean_styles, so every flow that reached the reset through get_dataset (dataset
  sync, style upload, replace) still resets GeoServer at least once;
  sync_instance_with_geoserver also keeps its own reset before reading the resource.
  One call of set_styles made three resets and now makes one.
- exists is called by GET /api/v2/resource-service/exists/<uuid> (any caller made
  GeoServer reset) and by the validation of append and replace, whose import resets
  GeoServer before it starts (_execute_resource_import in geoserver/manager.py).

Not changed: the other calls of reset() (upload, import, sync, clean_styles, purge of
a coverage store), which follow a change of data. The ones of sync and clean_styles
were removed afterwards by Inteligeo-load-4-geoserver-reset-on-sync.patch, whose
header supersedes the reading above for them.

Files touched: geonode/geoserver/helpers.py and geonode/geoserver/manager.py, not
among the files inteligeo-geonode copies over the installed package (its
src/inteligeo5/geoserver/manager.py is not copied by its Dockerfile).

Test run (2026-11-12): applied with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0 --no-backup-if-mismatch` on a
copy of site-packages/geonode taken from the 5.5.4 image (which already carries the
earlier patches of this folder), in the order the Dockerfile applies them: 2 files, 2 hunks, 0 FAILED,
0 fuzz; `python -m py_compile` clean. Not run in a pod: an upload, a replace with a changed schema and a style upload have to be repeated in the mirror, and the GeoServer log read for the dispose() message.

--- a/geonode/geoserver/helpers.py
+++ b/geonode/geoserver/helpers.py
@@ -1102,7 +1102,9 @@
 
 
 def get_dataset(layer, gs_catalog: Catalog):
-    gs_catalog.reset()
+    # Only the client's own cache is dropped. A POST /rest/reset here made GeoServer
+    # dispose its store connections on every read of a layer, under requests in flight.
+    gs_catalog._cache.clear()
     gs_dataset = None
     try:
         gs_dataset = gs_catalog.get_layer(layer.name)
